---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = "About – The Book of Life";
const description = "Robat Das Orvi writes about football tactics, AI systems, and the psychology of building things. On why those belong on the same site, and what connects the essays to the products.";
const currentPath = "/about";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} currentPath={currentPath}>
  <div class="simple-wrap">
    <div class="pg-title">About</div>
    <div class="pg-rule"></div>

    <div class="pg-bio">
      <p>These essays were written across three years, in airports, cafés, hotel rooms, and one football stadium. They were not written to be a book. They became one in retrospect, when I noticed the same questions surfacing in different clothes.</p>

      <p>The question underneath all of them: how do we pay attention to the right things? Technology automates it. Travel disrupts it. Startups consume it. Philosophy interrogates it. Football demands it for ninety minutes.</p>

      <p>Why football sits next to AI here is the thing people ask most, usually politely, in a tone suggesting I should pick one. The honest answer is that they are the same subject wearing different kit.</p>

      <p>A football team is the most legible complex system we have. Eleven people, one shared objective, constraints everyone can see, and a result at the end that cannot be spun. When I write about <a href="/chapters/football/the-half-space-the-10-metres-of-grass-that-changed-how-football-is-played">the half-space</a> or <a href="/chapters/football/gegenpressing-explained-why-every-team-copies-it-and-most-get-it-wrong">why teams copy gegenpressing badly</a>, I am writing about the same failure I see in software teams: a group adopting the visible shape of a solution without the mechanism that made it work.</p>

      <p>The tactical writing is also where I keep myself honest. Football punishes a bad theory in ninety minutes. Product opinions can survive for years without ever meeting evidence.</p>

      <p>The building runs alongside the writing. I co-created <a href="https://www.onethreadapp.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OneThread</a>, project management software for teams, and later <a href="https://agencyhandy.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AgencyHandy</a>, a system for client workflows built to reduce friction without removing judgment.</p>

      <p>More recently the work has moved toward guardrails for AI systems. <a href="https://noburn.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NoBurn</a> and <a href="https://baar.robatdasorvi.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">baar-core</a> block expensive LLM agent calls before they execute, and <a href="https://apidiffguard.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ApiDiffGuard</a> catches breaking API changes before they reach production.</p>

      <p>I did not plan that pattern, but it is unmistakable in hindsight. Every product I have built is a constraint. Something that stops an agent before it spends, catches a change before it ships, or keeps a workspace quiet enough to think in. I write essays about attention and then build software whose entire job is refusing things on your behalf. The essays and the products turn out to be the same argument: capability is cheap now, and judgment about what not to do is the scarce part.</p>

      <p>As for how these are written: claims carry sources, and when the evidence contradicts the thesis I would rather say so in the piece than quietly drop it. Several essays exist mainly to argue against a position I used to hold. If something is my opinion rather than a finding, I try to mark it as mine.</p>

      <p>I do not have a better answer to the attention question. But I find it worth asking every time.</p>

      <p class="pg-contact">Written by Robat Das Orvi. You can find me on <a href="https://twitter.com/orvi_onethread" target="_blank" rel="noopener">X</a> and <a href="https://github.com/orvi2014" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>, or read <a href="/chapters">the chapters</a>.</p>

      <div class="pg-sig">— Orvi</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .simple-wrap {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 52px 40px 100px;
  }

  .pg-title {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 42px;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  .pg-rule {
    width: 40px;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--sienna);
    margin-bottom: 36px;
  }

  .pg-bio {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1.82;
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 600px;
  }

  .pg-bio p {
    margin-bottom: 1.4em;
  }

  .pg-bio a {
    color: var(--sienna);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-color: rgba(139,94,60,0.35);
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .pg-contact {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--muted);
    padding-top: 1.2em;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .pg-sig {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--sienna);
    margin-top: 2em;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .simple-wrap { padding: 36px 24px 72px; }
    .pg-title { font-size: 32px; }
    .pg-bio { font-size: 17px; }
  }
</style>
